<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--

    Copyright (C) 2026 Antony Stubbs and contributors

    SpotBugs exclusions for the Java reference demo (astubbs#242). The gate itself is bound in the
    Java client aggregator's pom, which owns the policy: a dismissal is named class by class and
    pattern by pattern with the reason written beside it, and a module-wide or pattern-wide
    exclusion is the thing to refuse - it silences the finding for code nobody has written yet.

    WHY THIS FILE APPEARED. The demo predates this repository turning fb-contrib on with
    includeTests, so its findings arrived at merge rather than at write time. Each was read at the
    site before being dismissed; none is a mute for convenience, and every entry below names the
    ONE class it applies to.

    Every entry is a judgement, not a mute. If you are adding one, write why, or fix the code.

-->
<FindBugsFilter>

    <!--
        THE DEMO'S OUTPUT *IS* PRE-RENDERED TEXT, so "{}" alone is the correct format string rather
        than a lazy one. Three sites: the usage error, the banner-and-effective-configuration
        announcement, and the results table. Each hands slf4j a string that was built to be printed
        verbatim - a table with its own column alignment, a multi-line banner - so there is nothing
        to interpolate around it. The alternative fb-contrib is steering towards is passing the
        variable AS the format string, which is worse: a rendered table containing braces would then
        be parsed as placeholders.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="bz.stub.parallelconsumer.client.demo.ReferenceDemo"/>
        <Bug pattern="SLF4J_SIGN_ONLY_FORMAT"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
        A TABLE RENDERER READS THE ROWS IT RENDERS. CE_CLASS_ENVY fires on renderTable because it
        calls ArmResult's accessors more than its own - which is the definition of a renderer, and
        the alternative is moving presentation into the value type so that a record of a measurement
        also knows about column widths. Scoped to this class: a future method here that really is
        living in another class's data is still reported.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="bz.stub.parallelconsumer.client.demo.ReferenceDemo"/>
        <Bug pattern="CE_CLASS_ENVY"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
        baselineOf is PRIVATE and its one caller already holds a List. Widening the parameter to
        Collection would buy no caller anything and would lose the ordering the arms are reported
        in, which the list carries and a Collection does not promise.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="bz.stub.parallelconsumer.client.demo.ReferenceDemo"/>
        <Method name="baselineOf"/>
        <Bug pattern="OCP_OVERLY_CONCRETE_COLLECTION_PARAMETER"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
        THE LOCK IS THE POINT, AND IT IS ALREADY THE SECOND ANSWER TO A SPOTBUGS FINDING. The raw-gRPC
        control arm's workers all report through one StreamObserver, which is not safe for concurrent
        onNext, so they serialise on a dedicated `reportLock`. It was the AtomicReference that was
        synchronized on until JLM_JSR166_UTILCONCURRENT_MONITORENTER rightly rejected that; the
        comment at the site records the change. NOS_NON_OWNED_SYNCHRONIZATION now objects that the
        lock is a captured local rather than a field of the locking class - true, because the class
        is the anonymous StreamObserver, and a lock owned by the arm that creates both the observer
        and its workers is the correct scope. The regex covers the anonymous inner classes, whose
        numbering is not stable enough to name one by one.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="~bz\.stub\.parallelconsumer\.client\.demo\.ReferenceDemo(\$\d+)?"/>
        <Bug pattern="NOS_NON_OWNED_SYNCHRONIZATION"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
        A DEMO'S FAILURE MODE IS A SENTENCE THE READER CAN ACT ON, not a checked exception it makes
        them handle. Kafka's Admin and Producer futures throw ExecutionException; these three methods
        catch it and rethrow IllegalStateException naming what to do about it - which topic flag to
        pass, and with what value. Two of them hand e.getCause() straight through as the cause, so
        nothing is lost, and the third is the seeding failure, which carries the first producer
        exception it collected.

        (Flag names are deliberately not quoted here: a double hyphen cannot appear inside an XML
        comment, and SpotBugs reports an unreadable filter as a WARNING it then continues past - so
        the whole file goes silently unapplied and the gate simply keeps failing.)

        EXS_EXCEPTION_SOFTENING_NO_CONSTRAINTS is the right rule for a library, whose caller has to be
        able to distinguish failures programmatically. It is the wrong rule for a main() whose only
        caller is a person reading a terminal - and this module is a program, not a surface anything
        depends on. Named method by method so a new one has to be argued for.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="bz.stub.parallelconsumer.client.demo.DemoBroker"/>
        <Or>
            <Method name="ensureTopic"/>
            <Method name="partitionsOf"/>
            <Method name="seed"/>
        </Or>
        <Bug pattern="EXS_EXCEPTION_SOFTENING_NO_CONSTRAINTS"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
        THE EXCEPTION IN HAND IS NOT THE FAILURE BEING REPORTED. Inside the TopicExistsException
        catch, an existing topic is fine; what is thrown is a DIFFERENT finding made afterwards - the
        existing topic has the wrong partition count - and attaching "topic already exists" as its
        cause would point the reader at the thing that is working. The message names the mismatch and
        both remedies instead. Scoped to the one method and the one pattern.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="bz.stub.parallelconsumer.client.demo.DemoBroker"/>
        <Method name="ensureTopic"/>
        <Bug pattern="LEST_LOST_EXCEPTION_STACK_TRACE"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
        THE SAME TWO JUDGEMENTS, IN THE TEST TREE. `includeTests` is on for this repository because
        test code is main code for a concurrency library, and these two are the test-side instances
        of the rules already answered above.

        awaitQuietly is a test helper whose entire job is to turn InterruptedException into a
        failure the test can report; a checked signature would push the same conversion into every
        caller. ReferenceDemoIT's assertion lambda reads an ArmResult's accessors, which is what
        asserting about a value type looks like.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="bz.stub.parallelconsumer.client.demo.ArmCompletionTest"/>
        <Method name="awaitQuietly"/>
        <Bug pattern="EXS_EXCEPTION_SOFTENING_NO_CONSTRAINTS"/>
    </Match>
    <Match>
        <Class name="bz.stub.parallelconsumer.client.demo.integrationTests.ReferenceDemoIT"/>
        <Bug pattern="CE_CLASS_ENVY"/>
    </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
